The best move was the immediate f3, which came to my intuition but I wanted to kill all counterplay before proceeding with my f3 break, however the counterplay on the queenside was fake, and it was stronger to break open the kingside before his pieces coordinate well. }  (16. f3  b4 {I was worried about that terrible light squared bishop becoming active on the a3 f1 diagonal, and from a human perspective this is logical, however, I needed to recognise the demands of the position, that his kindside pawns are overextended and king is somewhat bare. Furthermore his b4 ba6 idea will take some tempi which I can use to bring pieces to the kingside and attack. If he gives me time the g4 pawn can be weak and isolated after the trades. }  (16... gxf3  17. Bxf3 b4 18. Rf2) 17. fxg4 fxg4 18. Qd1) a4 {[%eval +2.02] }  (16... Nf8  17. f3 Bg5 18. Rf2 axb4 19. Rb1 Ng6 20. Rb3 Rf8 21. Nxb4) 17. Nf4 {[%eval +1.85] }  (17. f3  ) Nf8 {[%eval +2.23] } 18. Ned3 {[%eval +2.07] } Ng6 {[%eval +2.12] } 19. f3 {[%eval +1.74] } gxf3 {[%eval +2.22] } 20. Bxf3 {[%eval +2.40] } Nxf4 {[%eval +2.30] } 21. Nxf4 {[%eval +2.26] } Kh8 {[%eval +2.31] } 22. Qg2 {[%eval +2.42] } Rg8 {[%eval +2.40] } 23. Qh3 {[%eval +2.21] } Qe8 {[%eval +2.46] } 24. Bd2 {[%eval +2.44] } Bd7 {[%eval +2.55] } 25. Kh1 {[%eval +2.54] } Qf7 {[%eval +2.55] }  (25... Qf8  ) 26. Rf2 {[%eval +2.46] } Raf8 {[%eval +2.33] } 27. Be2 {[%eval +2.20] }  (27. Raf1  ) Be8 {[%eval +2.74] } 28. Qh6 {[%eval +2.50] } Bd8?! {[%eval +3.53] Inaccuracy. Bd7 was best.}  (28... Bd7  29. Rg1 Ne8 30. Bh5 Qg7 31. Ng6+ Qxg6 32. Bxg6 Rxg6 33. Qe3 Ng7 34. h3) 29. Raf1 {[%eval +2.97] } Qe7 {[%eval +2.98] } 30. Qh3 {[%eval +2.37] This was a bit of a panic move, I was starting to get slightly low on time and definitely wanted to avoid a queen trade, he was threatening Qg5. Perhaps it was better to go h4, stopping Qg5 ideas, but I was scared of weakening my g pawn. However concretely this was nothing to be worried about. taking no risk is sometimes riskier than taking risk. }  (30. h4! {[%cal Gh4h5,Gf4g6,Gg3g4]I was worried about my queen getting weirdly trapped on h6 and my g pawn being weak, so why is h4 so good? perhaps the idea is to go nh3 and ng4? }  Bf7  (30... Bh5  31. Bxh5 Nxh5 32. Nxh5 Qf7)  (30... Nh5  31. Nxh5 Bxh5 32. Bxh5 Rxg3 33. Rh2) 31. h5 {[%cal Gf4g6]the idea is to go h5 and then possibly ng6+ which is crushing. Also g4 ideas in the air too to open the doubled rooks. } Ne8 32. g4 Nc7) Bf7 {[%eval +2.35] } 31. Bd3 {[%eval +2.13] }  (31. Rg1  ) Qe8 {[%eval +2.43] } 32. g4! {[%eval +2.31] great pawn break, he can't take because of mate}  (32. Rg1  Nh5 33. Be2 Nxf4 34. Bxf4 Rg7) Qe7 {[%eval +2.90] } 33. g5?! {[%eval +1.66] Inaccuracy. Ng2 was best. This is way too slow ng2! was much better, preventing a queen trade on h4 and also adding 2 extra attackers onto the f5 pawn! I briefly considered this button was bias in my evaluation because it seemed like a weird choice to go backwards with my knight from such a beautiful outpost which was preventing his minor pieces from entering, but in this case it's concretely the strongest. }  (33. Ng2  Bg6 34. Qh6 Ne8 35. Nf4 Rf7 36. Rg2 Rfg7 37. Rg3 Nc7 38. Rfg1 Be8) Qe8 {[%eval +1.56] } 34. Rg1 {[%eval +1.03] } Nh5 {[%eval +1.08] } 35. Be2 {[%eval +1.08] } Nxf4 {[%eval +1.08] } 36. Bxf4 {[%eval +0.89] } Rg7 {[%eval +1.02] } 37. Qf3?! {[%eval +0.33] Inaccuracy. Rg3 was best.}  (37. Rg3  Rfg8 38. Rfg2 Bg6 39. Rg1 Rb7 40. Kg2 Kg7 41. Qh6+ Kh8 42. Rh1 Be7) Rfg8 {[%eval +0.36] } 38. Rfg2 {[%eval +0.50] }  (38. h4  Rg6 39. h5 Rxg5 40. Bxg5 Rxg5 41. Rxg5 Bxg5 42. h6 Bxh6 43. Qh3 Bg5 44. Rg2 h6  (44... Qe7  45. Bh5 Bg8)  (44... Qd8  45. Bh5 Bg8)) Be7 {[%eval +0.70] } 39. h4 {[%eval +0.44] } Qd8 {[%eval +0.41] } 40. Rg3 {[%eval +0.51] } Rg6?? {[%eval +2.86] Blunder. h6 was best. I knew this was a mistake, I thought "he can't take with the rook i'd win an exchange but I didn't properly evaluate the consequences. saccing the exchange for a minor piece and a crucial pawn is definitely worth it for him. Here, I needed to stay patient and repositioin my queen to the h file to prepare h5, but instead I rushed and gave away my advantage. }  (40... h6  41. Rh3 Qe8 42. Rg2 h5 43. g6 Bxg6 44. Bh6 Rh7 45. Qf4 Bf7 46. Rxg8+) 41. h5?? {[%eval +0.07] Blunder. Qg2 was best.}  (41. Qg2 {[%cal Gg2h3,Gh4h5,Gg5g6]now because he went rg3 which is a waiting move, he sheds a tempo and can't react to h5 in time with Qe8 because he must spend a tempo moving his rook off that diagonal which gives me time. 

